{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 and Mathlib formalization of an exact decision procedure, on an explicit word RAM, for the connectivity of the perfect matchings of a hexagonal plane graph under hexagonal face flips. A hexagonal plane graph is a finite simple graph with a rotation system satisfying Euler's formula |V| + |F| = |E| + 2 (F the set of faces), a designated outer face and a designated set of other faces (the defects), such that every remaining face is a hexagon, bounded by six darts with six distinct tails; the graph is 2-connected, every vertex has degree 2 or 3, and a vertex of degree 2 lies on the outer face or on a defect. A hexagonal face flip replaces a perfect matching M, in which a hexagonal face alternates, by M with the matched and unmatched edges of that face exchanged; the graph is flip connected if every perfect matching is reached from every other by hexagonal face flips. One theorem is compared: there are six fixed word programs for a word RAM (unit cost per instruction, natural registers and memory cells bounded by a word bound W along the run; the machine also has exact real registers, and a word program contains no instruction that uses them) such that (i) isPrime, on the input [q], prints [1] if q is prime and [0] otherwise, within 8 floor(sqrt q) + 16 steps whenever W >= 4q + 8; (ii) for every hexagonal plane graph with n vertices and edge set E and every numbering of its faces, darts and vertices, orient (on a fixed encoding of the rotation system), ray (on that encoding and the number of a defect) and, for every prime p, defect and z = 1 or z = -1, the chain signs, build, detMod (each reading what the previous program printed) run within the stated step and word bounds (225n + 99 and 189n + 86 steps for orient and ray; 84n + 18, 9n^2 + 66n + 21 and 11n^3 + 37n^2 + 12n floor(log2 p) + 62n + 17 steps for the chain), the chain printing one word; (iii) the first 2|E| + 3 primes are at most 16 (2|E| + 3)^2; and (iv) the graph is flip connected exactly when, for each of the first 2|E| + 3 primes, the words printed for z = 1 and z = -1 agree for every defect. The programs compute the determinants, modulo p, of the signed adjacency matrices of a Kasteleyn orientation with the edges crossed by the ray weighted by z; the theorem itself states only the runs and the exact decision. The proofs use only the axioms propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound.",
